<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Tukaroi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1575">1575</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Mughalmari</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Tukaroi was fought on 3 March 1575 near the village of Tukaroi in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Balasore</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the Mughal army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> under Munim Khan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a>, sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Daud was defeated and signed the Treaty of Katak, giving up Bengal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, but he soon renewed the war and was finally defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a> in 1576.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-tukaroi.jpg" alt="Battle of Tukaroi" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manohar, 1590 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Tukaroi_(3_March_1575).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a>'s seizure of Fort Zamania, a frontier post of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> cause for war. Munim Khan, sent against him, granted Daud lenient terms after some skirmishing near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a>, which displeased Akbar, while Daud killed his own minister, Lodi Khan. When Munim Khan besieged Patna and found the task beyond him, Akbar came in person, embarking on 15 June 1574 for a voyage down the rivers at the height of the rainy season.</p><p>In August 1574 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s men took Hajipur, on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> depended for supplies, and Daud slipped out of the city by night and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, leaving booty that included 265 elephants. Akbar left an army of more than 20,000 under Munim Khan, appointed governor of Bengal, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Raja Todar Mal</a> under him, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatehpur-sikri">Fatehpur Sikri</a> on 18 January 1575. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> entered the Bengal capital, Tanda, and Daud withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Munim Khan was forced to fight before he was ready when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> attacked at dawn. A premature charge by the Mughal advance guard under Alam Khan, and Munim Khan's order recalling it, threw the Mughal front into disorder. Daud's elephants could not be stopped even by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire, and his general Gujar Khan broke through to Munim Khan and wounded him.</p><p>While the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> began looting the Mughal tents, Kia Khan and other commanders regrouped, and Gujar Khan was killed by a gunshot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a> and Shaham Khan then defeated the Afghan wings, and Daud fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>. Many Afghans were captured; the captives were killed and eight towers built from their remains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>At Cuttack Daud offered a treaty, which the war-weary Munim Khan signed despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a>'s objection. Daud submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and kept only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> as a Mughal vassal. The treaty failed after Munim Khan's death, when Daud invaded Bengal again. The battle is regarded in Bengal as the moment the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> lost their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Tukaroi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Daud Khan Karrani defied the Mughals, was defeated at <b>Tukaroi</b> in 1575 and was captured and executed after the Battle of Rajmahal in July 1576, ending the sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
